u}_\ell$ transitions to include the full set of dimension-six, Lorentz-invariant operators with left-handed neutrinos, consistent with the Standard Model Effective Field Theory (SMEFT) framework. The Wilson coefficients are treated as complex, lepton-flavor-dependent effective couplings. Since the three selected channels are sensitive to different non-standard structures, we perform a combined analysis using Monte Carlo methods to constrain the New Physics couplings for three benchmark values of $|V_{ub}|$. We find that, while the Standard Model remains broadly compatible with the data, some of the couplings show a mild preference for non-zero values.

hep-ph.abstract:
In this article, we reanalyze the mass and pole residue of $\Lambda$-type singly heavy baryons with spin-parity $J^P=1/2^{\pm}$ using two-point QCD sum rules. Then, we investigate the electroweak transition form factors of $\Lambda_b	o\Lambda^*_c(2595)$ and $\Xi_b	o\Xi^*_c(2790)$ within the framework of three-point QCD sum rules. At the hadron side, all possible couplings of the interpolating currents to hadronic states are taken into account, while at the QCD side, both the perturbative part and vacuum condensate contributions up to dimension-8 are included. Using the obtained form factors, we calculate the decay widths and branching ratios for the semileptonic decays $\Lambda_b	o\Lambda^*_c(2595)\ell ar{
u}_\ell$ and $\Xi_b	o\Xi^*_c(2790)\ell ar{
u}_\ell$ ($\ell=e,\mu$ and $	au$), as well as for the corresponding nonleptonic decays $\Lambda_b	o\Lambda^*_c(2595)\pi[ho]$, $\Lambda_b	o\Lambda^*_c(2595)K[K^*]$, $\Xi_b	o\Xi^*_c(2790)\pi[ho]$ and $\Xi_b	o\Xi^*_c(2790)K[K^*]$. Our estimates for the branching ratios of $\Lambda_b	o\Lambda^*_c(2595)\ell ar{
u}_\ell$ are comparable with the experimental data and other theoretical results. These results can provide valuable references for future measurements. In addition, we present predictions for several observables in these semileptonic decays, including the lepton universality ratio, the forward-backward asymmetry $A_{FB}$, and the asymmetry parameter $\alpha$, which can provide useful references for future experiments to test the Standard Model and probe new physics beyond it.

